import{_ as a,o as n,c as t,a0 as i}from"./chunks/framework.TJhnt8t4.js";const g=JSON.parse('{"title":"Implementing your own connector","description":"","frontmatter":{},"headers":[],"relativePath":"en/guide/custom-connector.md","filePath":"en/guide/custom-connector.md"}'),o={name:"en/guide/custom-connector.md"};function s(r,e,c,d,l,p){return n(),t("div",null,[...e[0]||(e[0]=[i(`<h1 id="implementing-your-own-connector" tabindex="-1">Implementing your own connector <a class="header-anchor" href="#implementing-your-own-connector" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Implementing your own connector&quot;">​</a></h1><p>Using the <a href="./android-sdk">Android</a> or <a href="./ios-macos-sdk">iOS/macOS</a> SDK isn&#39;t required. The protocol they implement is public and documented here — a client can fetch the registry, verify it, and apply the pins with whatever HTTP and TLS stack it already uses. This is the same specification both official SDKs follow; see <a href="./architecture">Architecture</a> for the full cryptographic rationale.</p><h2 id="what-your-connector-needs-to-do" tabindex="-1">What your connector needs to do <a class="header-anchor" href="#what-your-connector-needs-to-do" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;What your connector needs to do&quot;">​</a></h2><ol><li><strong>Fetch</strong> the registry over HTTPS: <code>GET</code> the configured endpoint, parse the top-level JSON object — <code>payload</code> and <code>signature</code>. Capture the raw JSON substring of <code>payload</code> exactly as received, <strong>before</strong> any deserialization. This byte-for-byte string is what the signature actually covers; re-serializing it (different key order, different whitespace) will invalidate verification.</li><li><strong>Canonicalize</strong> that raw string according to <a href="https://datatracker.ietf.org/doc/html/rfc8785" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">JCS, RFC 8785</a>: sort object keys by Unicode code point, remove insignificant whitespace, apply the standard&#39;s number and string serialization rules.</li><li><strong>Hash</strong> the canonicalized bytes with SHA-512.</li><li><strong>Verify</strong> the <code>signature</code> field (Base64-decoded) against that hash using RSA PKCS#1 v1.5, with the public key embedded in your app (the <code>pub.pem</code> generated alongside your server&#39;s signing key — see <a href="./architecture#generating-signing-keys">Generating signing keys</a>). Accept the public key in whichever format is convenient for your embedding strategy: PEM, Base64-encoded PEM, or raw DER (SPKI) — both official SDKs support all three for exactly this reason.</li><li><strong>Fail closed.</strong> If the signature doesn&#39;t verify, if the endpoint is unreachable, or the response isn&#39;t well-formed, do not create a pinned client and do not fall back to an unpinned one silently. Abort initialization or enter a degraded, user-visible mode. Neither official SDK ever produces a partially-verified or best-effort pinned client — there is no safe middle ground here.</li><li><strong>Apply the pins.</strong> Once verified, deserialize <code>payload.keys</code> and configure your HTTP client&#39;s certificate/public-key pinning with the SHA-256 SPKI hash from each entry&#39;s <code>key</code> field, scoped to its <code>domainName</code>. See <a href="./architecture#signed-registry-format">Signed registry format</a> for the exact field meanings.</li></ol><div class="language- vp-adaptive-theme"><button title="Copy Code" class="copy"></button><span class="lang"></span><pre class="shiki shiki-themes github-light github-dark vp-code" tabindex="0"><code><span class="line"><span>your app</span></span>
<span class="line"><span>   │</span></span>
<span class="line"><span>   ├─ GET &lt;endpoint&gt;                         → { payload, signature }</span></span>
<span class="line"><span>   ├─ canonicalize(raw payload string)  [JCS]</span></span>
<span class="line"><span>   ├─ sha512(canonical)</span></span>
<span class="line"><span>   ├─ verify(signature, hash, embedded pubkey)  [RSA PKCS#1 v1.5]</span></span>
<span class="line"><span>   │    ├─ fail  → abort / degraded mode, no pinned client</span></span>
<span class="line"><span>   │    └─ pass  → parse payload.keys</span></span>
<span class="line"><span>   │                for each key: pin sha256(SPKI) to domainName</span></span>
<span class="line"><span>   │                in your TLS/HTTP stack&#39;s pinning mechanism</span></span>
<span class="line"><span>   └─ done</span></span></code></pre></div><h2 id="why-this-is-safe-to-do-yourself" tabindex="-1">Why this is safe to do yourself <a class="header-anchor" href="#why-this-is-safe-to-do-yourself" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Why this is safe to do yourself&quot;">​</a></h2><p>The registry&#39;s signature is the only thing standing between a tampered response and a trusted one — it&#39;s a standard RSA signature over a standard canonicalization scheme, not a proprietary protocol. Any platform with an RSA-signature-verification primitive and a conformant JCS implementation can validate it correctly. The two official SDKs exist for convenience (HTTP client wiring, pinning API integration) — not because the protocol itself requires platform-specific trust.</p><h2 id="where-to-get-the-moving-pieces" tabindex="-1">Where to get the moving pieces <a class="header-anchor" href="#where-to-get-the-moving-pieces" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Where to get the moving pieces&quot;">​</a></h2><ul><li>The registry endpoint and response shape: <a href="./self-hosting">Self-hosting</a> and <a href="./../reference/http-api">HTTP API</a>.</li><li>The exact JSON schema you&#39;re canonicalizing and verifying: <a href="./architecture#signed-registry-format">Signed registry format</a>.</li><li>A worked, language-specific reference implementation of every step above: the <a href="./android-sdk">Android SDK</a> source (Kotlin) and the <a href="./ios-macos-sdk">Apple SDK</a> source (Swift) are both open — reading either one is a faster way to get the byte-level details right than re-deriving RFC 8785 from scratch.</li></ul>`,9)])])}const f=a(o,[["render",s]]);export{g as __pageData,f as default};
